<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  RemoteDrawSenderKit privacy manifest.

  This is a third-party SDK: everything below becomes part of the *host app's*
  privacy report, and the host's developer has to defend it to Apple without
  having read our source. So every entry here is traceable to a named symbol in
  this package, and nothing is declared "to be safe" — an over-declaration is as
  wrong as an omission, because it becomes an App Store Connect answer somebody
  else has to justify about code they did not write.

  Scope: this manifest covers both targets in the package. `RemoteDrawInk` is
  not separately declarable — it has no manifest of its own and a customer
  cannot take the renderer without the sender — so its one required-reason API
  (`RDTrace.measure`, below) is declared here.

  Signature: Apple's manifest-plus-signature requirement applies to SDKs on its
  commonly-used list. RemoteDrawSenderKit is not on that list, and a source
  SwiftPM package cannot be signed in any case — signatures apply to binary
  frameworks. The manifest is required in substance regardless, because the host
  app's privacy report has to account for what we do.

  What is deliberately NOT here, and why:

  - Camera. Session initiation from a QR code is not in this package: it takes
    a token as a string and opens no capture device, so it forces no
    `NSCameraUsageDescription` on the host — the property the README leads with,
    because it is the difference between a two-hour integration and a two-week
    one. The surface that arrived in Stage 2 did not change that: the default
    configuration still forces **zero** Info.plist keys.
  - Local network / Bonjour. `.nearbyLocalNetwork` is not in this SDK. Shipping
    an advertiser would cost every host two Info.plist declarations for a
    broadcast that today's primary receiver — a web page, which cannot do DNS-SD
    at all — could not hear.
  - File timestamp, disk space, active keyboard. No code in either target
    touches them.
  - Location. There is no CoreLocation import and no authorization request. Map
    boards get their region from the board's own target metadata, and the map
    ground stays in the first-party app rather than shipping here.
  - Crash and performance data. Nothing in this package reports anywhere. The
    host's own diagnostics SDK declares its own.
  - Contacts, photos, microphone, health, purchases, search or browsing history,
    sensitive info. None are read.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  No tracking. No IDFA, no ATT prompt, no AppTrackingTransparency import, and
	  nothing this SDK collects is combined with data from other companies' apps
	  or sites for advertising or measurement. Confirmed by grep: neither
	  `advertisingIdentifier` nor `ATTrackingManager` appears in either target,
	  and the package has zero external dependencies, so there is no transitive
	  code to account for.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Empty because the only host this SDK contacts is the RemoteDraw API the
	  integrator configured (`RemoteDrawAPIBaseURL`), which is first-party
	  service-provider traffic for app functionality. There is no analytics
	  endpoint, no telemetry collector, and no second origin.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  Other user content: the drawing itself. Stroke points and their
		  pressure/tilt channels (`/v1/sender/draft`, `/commit`, `/replace`),
		  any text annotation, and the phone's projection rectangle.

		  A stroke is whatever the person drew — handwriting, an annotation, a
		  diagram, a mark on somebody's document — so this is user content, not
		  usage data, and calling it anything else would understate it.

		  NOT LINKED, and that is a real consequence of the SDK's design rather
		  than a convenient answer: this SDK authenticates no end user. It holds
		  a session-scoped `rd_send_…` capability and no account, no email, no
		  sign-in. There is nothing on our side for the content to be linked
		  *to*. A host that separately knows who its own user is has linked it in
		  their own app, and declares that themselves.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Device ID. `RemoteDrawSenderDevice.current()` reads
		  `UIDevice.current.identifierForVendor` into `SenderDevice.deviceId`,
		  which rides on `/v1/join`. It is how a board tells one connected phone
		  from another.

		  OPT-OUT, and the opt-out is a first-class API rather than a documented
		  workaround: `RemoteDrawSenderDevice.anonymous(aspectRatio:screen:)`
		  omits the key from the request body entirely — not an empty string, not
		  a zero UUID — so a host that chooses it collects no identifier at all
		  and can delete this entry from its own report. A test
		  (`CaptureTests.testAnAnonymousDeviceSendsGeometryAndNoIdentifier`)
		  holds that promise to the encoded JSON.

		  Not linked, for the same reason as the content above: no account.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  Other data: the device description the board needs in order to draw
		  correctly. `RemoteDrawSenderDevice` also carries `platform`,
		  `appVersion`, `displayName` (`UIDevice.current.name` — the model name,
		  not a user-chosen one, absent the user-assigned-device-name
		  entitlement), `modelIdentifier` (`uname`), `aspectRatio`, and a
		  `RemoteDrawSenderDeviceScreen` of points, scale, pixel dimensions,
		  safe-area insets and cutout kind.

		  The receiver uses these to place this phone's viewport on the board and
		  to size the ink. None of it fits one of Apple's named categories, and
		  it is not diagnostics — it is the geometry the product runs on — so it
		  lands in Other Data.

		  Also covered here: `RemoteDrawNormalizedPoint.t`, milliseconds since
		  process start, which feeds stroke velocity. See the boot-time
		  declaration below for why it is measured the way it is.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  User defaults — CA92.1, "access info from same app, per documentation".

		  Added by Stage 2, which is when the first `UserDefaults` access in this
		  package shipped, exactly as the Stage 1 manifest said it would be.
		  `RemoteDrawSurface` stores five preferences through `@AppStorage`
		  (`RemoteDrawPreferences`): handedness, instrument, thickness, colour and
		  whether shapes fill.

		  CA92.1 and not CA92.5 (app-group) or CA92.4 (managed-app
		  configuration): every one of these is written by this SDK into the host
		  app's own defaults domain and read back by this SDK in the same app.
		  There is no suite name, no app group, and nothing is read that the SDK
		  did not write.

		  It changes nothing in the collected-data list above. A person's choice
		  of pencil never leaves the device: it is not transmitted, it is not an
		  identifier, and it is not combined with anything. A host that would
		  rather own the storage passes its own bindings and this declaration
		  still stands, because the surface's own defaults are what it falls back
		  to.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>

		<!--
		  System boot time — 35F9.1, "measure the amount of time that has elapsed
		  between events that occurred within the app, or perform calculations to
		  enable timers."

		  Two uses, in two targets:

		  - `RemoteDrawInkClock` (StrokeTransport.swift), the clock behind
		    `RemoteDrawNormalizedPoint.t`, which feeds stroke velocity and
		    therefore ribbon width.
		  - `RDTrace.measure` (RemoteDrawInk/InkTracing.swift), which times the
		    paper and tooth rasterisations. Console only; never transmitted.

		  THE SUBTLETY THAT MUST SURVIVE EVERY FUTURE SIMPLIFICATION: `t` *is*
		  transmitted, and 35F9.1 forbids sending boot time or anything derived
		  from it off-device — except "information about the amount of time that
		  has elapsed between events that occurred within the app."
		  `RemoteDrawInkClock` subtracts a process-start origin precisely so that
		  every transmitted `t` sits inside that exception. A bare
		  `ProcessInfo.systemUptime` would not, and would make this declaration a
		  false statement in every host app that links us.

		  It is not only a privacy constraint. The packed codec quantises this
		  channel into an `Int32`, and an epoch-scale timestamp overflows it —
		  which is the defect that killed the first-party app on the first point
		  of the first stroke. Both reasons point at the same implementation.
		  Do not change it back to a bare `systemUptime`.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
